WEBVTT

NOTE From evidence to forecast

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.833
A woman of forty goes for routine breast screening. The test comes back positive.

2
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:13.226
In a study reported in 1982, ninety-five of a hundred physicians put her chance of

3
00:00:13.226 --> 00:00:16.267
cancer at seventy to eighty percent.

4
00:00:16.733 --> 00:00:20.051
The right answer is under eight percent.

5
00:00:20.051 --> 00:00:23.867
Same evidence, and a number ten times too big.

6
00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:28.933
So where does a probability come from, and how can you tell whether it is any good?

7
00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:34.858
A probability is a summary of evidence, not a fact about the world.

8
00:00:34.858 --> 00:00:40.500
So it can be built badly, it can claim too much, and it can be checked.

9
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:47.289
Five stops: what the number counts, how evidence should move it, when one number is

10
00:00:47.289 --> 00:00:52.433
too many, what it says about the future, and how we know it was good.

11
00:00:52.833 --> 00:00:56.833
To see where the doctors went wrong, start with what the number counts.

12
00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:01.434
Counting needs a reference class, the group you count over.

13
00:01:01.434 --> 00:01:06.829
The philosopher Alan Hájek is a man, a non-smoker and a philosophy professor.

14
00:01:06.829 --> 00:01:11.033
Each group gives a different chance that he lives to eighty.

15
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:18.775
In 1814 Laplace counted five thousand years of sunrises, and put the odds of one

16
00:01:18.775 --> 00:01:21.867
more at nearly two million to one.

17
00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:28.941
Anyone who knows what drives the days and seasons, he added, would put the odds far

18
00:01:28.941 --> 00:01:33.400
higher. Same sunrise, more evidence, a different number.

19
00:01:33.867 --> 00:01:39.500
A probability counts over a group. Name the group, or the number floats.

20
00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:44.867
The group matters. But the doctors had the right group, and missed.

21
00:01:45.333 --> 00:01:48.798
Their error was how the test moved the number.

22
00:01:48.798 --> 00:01:54.900
Bayes' rule starts from the base rate, how common the disease is before any test.

23
00:01:55.367 --> 00:02:00.303
Take a thousand women of forty in screening. Ten have breast cancer.

24
00:02:00.303 --> 00:02:01.900
That is the base rate.

25
00:02:02.367 --> 00:02:04.745
Eight of those ten test positive.

26
00:02:04.745 --> 00:02:10.367
Of the nine hundred and ninety without cancer, ninety-five also test positive.

27
00:02:10.833 --> 00:02:16.632
So one hundred and three women test positive, and only eight of them have cancer.

28
00:02:16.632 --> 00:02:19.067
That is seven point eight percent.

29
00:02:19.467 --> 00:02:24.209
The disease is rare, so most positives come from healthy women.

30
00:02:24.209 --> 00:02:30.457
The doctors left out the base rate, as earlier research found people do most of the

31
00:02:30.457 --> 00:02:30.833
time.

32
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:32.214
Counts help.

33
00:02:32.214 --> 00:02:38.459
In Gigerenzer and Hoffrage's 1995 study, correct answers rose from sixteen percent

34
00:02:38.459 --> 00:02:41.733
with percentages to about half with counts.

35
00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:47.358
Start from how common it is, then let the test move you.

36
00:02:47.358 --> 00:02:50.767
Rare things stay rare after one test.

37
00:02:51.367 --> 00:02:55.633
Bayes gives one number, and for the test that was enough.

38
00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:59.087
But one number can claim too much.

39
00:02:59.087 --> 00:03:04.972
A coin that landed heads in half of a hundred tosses gets one half.

40
00:03:04.972 --> 00:03:11.033
So may a coin never seen. Its honest summary is a range, zero to one.

41
00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:18.448
Daniel Ellsberg's urn, from 1961, holds thirty red balls, and sixty black and yellow

42
00:03:18.448 --> 00:03:20.433
balls in an unknown mix.

43
00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.477
The chance of red is one third.

44
00:03:23.477 --> 00:03:28.133
The chance of black is anywhere from zero to two thirds.

45
00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:34.233
When evidence is thin, an honest summary is a range, not a point.

46
00:03:34.833 --> 00:03:40.567
A range is honest about today. A forecast reaches into tomorrow.

47
00:03:41.033 --> 00:03:43.260
That claim leans on the past.

48
00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:49.478
In 1748 David Hume wrote that every conclusion from experience assumes the future

49
00:03:49.478 --> 00:03:51.167
will be like the past.

50
00:03:51.633 --> 00:03:53.670
It also leans on a model.

51
00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:59.781
In 1963 Edward Lorenz showed that in a simple model of rising air, slightly

52
00:03:59.781 --> 00:04:04.100
different starts can grow into very different states.

53
00:04:04.567 --> 00:04:09.958
So Europe's main weather centre runs its model fifty times from slightly altered

54
00:04:09.958 --> 00:04:13.732
starts. When the runs agree, the weather is predictable.

55
00:04:13.732 --> 00:04:16.967
When they scatter, no firm forecast is possible.

56
00:04:17.433 --> 00:04:23.836
A forecast starts from the present. A projection holds only if its assumption holds.

57
00:04:23.836 --> 00:04:28.867
And a scenario is a plausible story, with no probability attached.

58
00:04:29.333 --> 00:04:33.008
A forecast leans on the past and on a model.

59
00:04:33.008 --> 00:04:37.267
Say which kind of claim it is, and show the spread.

60
00:04:37.867 --> 00:04:42.700
So a forecast is a claim, and a claim can be graded.

61
00:04:43.167 --> 00:04:49.860
The Brier score, from 1950, is the squared gap between forecast and outcome, and

62
00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:51.198
lower is better.

63
00:04:51.198 --> 00:04:58.142
In the Good Judgment Project, ninety percent cost zero point zero two if right, one

64
00:04:58.142 --> 00:05:00.067
point six two if wrong.

65
00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:02.459
One miss can be bad luck.

66
00:05:02.459 --> 00:05:08.931
Over many forecasts the scores show calibration: the events you call seventy percent

67
00:05:08.931 --> 00:05:12.167
should happen seventy percent of the time.

68
00:05:12.633 --> 00:05:14.422
People often are not.

69
00:05:14.422 --> 00:05:21.320
In studies reviewed by Tversky and Kahneman in 1974, ranges given as ninety-eight

70
00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:25.833
percent sure missed about thirty percent of the time.

71
00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:32.333
One outcome proves nothing. Many forecasts, scored, do.

72
00:05:32.933 --> 00:05:37.141
A probability counts over a group, so name the group.

73
00:05:37.141 --> 00:05:42.062
Evidence moves it by Bayes' rule, so start from the base rate.

74
00:05:42.062 --> 00:05:44.603
But thin evidence earns a range.

75
00:05:44.603 --> 00:05:50.794
A forecast adds a model, so say what kind of claim it is, and show the spread.

76
00:05:50.794 --> 00:05:54.367
And none of it is trusted until it is scored.

77
00:05:54.867 --> 00:05:56.820
Back to the positive test.

78
00:05:56.820 --> 00:06:02.678
Seven point eight percent counts a thousand women like her, moved by one test.

79
00:06:02.678 --> 00:06:06.133
It is one number because the evidence is good.

80
00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:13.222
So where does a probability come from, and how can you tell whether it is any good?

81
00:06:13.222 --> 00:06:18.567
From a named group, moved by evidence. And by scoring many like it.

82
00:06:19.067 --> 00:06:21.161
A scored number can still fail.

83
00:06:21.161 --> 00:06:26.767
The next video shows how: the listener hears a number other than the one you meant.
